WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
Step number three, edit the audio to only include the sermon and make any needed adjustments.

00:06.000 --> 00:10.000
There are multiple options for software to edit audio files.

00:10.000 --> 00:15.000
In this video, we will use the software called Audacity.

00:15.000 --> 00:20.000
Audacity is a free program available for Windows, Mac, and Linux.

00:20.000 --> 00:25.000
It can be downloaded from the website AudacityTeam.org.

00:25.000 --> 00:33.000
On the Audacity website, select Downloads at the top of the page.

00:33.000 --> 00:38.000
Then download the version of the software for the operating system you're using.

00:38.000 --> 00:42.000
You will also want to download the FF-MPEG library for Audacity.

00:42.000 --> 00:51.000
Click the link under FF-MPEG library for the software and instructions.

00:51.000 --> 00:58.000
Once you have downloaded and installed Audacity and FF-MPEG, run the Audacity program.

00:58.000 --> 01:10.000
To edit a downloaded audio file from the program menu, select File, then Import, then Audio.

01:10.000 --> 01:15.000
Audacity can import audio from both video and audio file formats.

01:15.000 --> 01:28.000
Here, we will select the MP4 video file downloaded from YouTube.

01:28.000 --> 01:34.000
It may take a couple minutes for Audacity to load the audio from a video file.

01:34.000 --> 01:40.000
When the import is finished, you will see two channels of audio for the stereo recording made of the service.

01:40.000 --> 01:45.000
To make things easier for editing, and because we really don't need stereo for what is usually one person,

01:45.000 --> 02:01.000
and a spoken word recording, convert the audio from stereo to mono by selecting Tracks, then Mix, then Mix stereo down to mono.

02:01.000 --> 02:05.000
Now that we have a single channel, we will normalize the volume.

02:05.000 --> 02:18.000
Select Effect, and then Normalize from the menu.

02:18.000 --> 02:29.000
Check the first two boxes, and enter minus 2, next to DB, and click Apply.

02:29.000 --> 02:34.000
Normalizing increases the overall volume without clipping or distorting it.

02:34.000 --> 02:39.000
This will make the audio easier to edit and also improve the quality.

02:39.000 --> 02:43.000
At this point, we have the audio for the entire service.

02:43.000 --> 02:47.000
Next, we need to find the beginning and end of the sermon within the service.

02:47.000 --> 02:54.000
Most of the time, the sermon begins after the gospel reading, and the end of the sermon is followed by the nice scene creed.

02:54.000 --> 02:59.000
We can click within the audio and press the Play button to listen to the various parts of the service.

02:59.000 --> 03:06.000
The toolbar buttons that look like a magnifying glass with plus and minus can be used to zoom in and out on the audio.

03:06.000 --> 03:14.000
Now, let's browse through the audio, searching for the start of the sermon, zooming in as necessary, and select the starting point.

03:14.000 --> 03:22.000
The spacebar can also be used to start and stop the playback, and that is what we will use to test where we are as we click on various parts of the service.

03:30.000 --> 03:39.000
There's King Shell C in a row.

03:39.000 --> 03:46.000
Is both a former international mission.

03:46.000 --> 04:01.000
Now, the Lord says, he who formed me from the womb to be his servant.

04:02.000 --> 04:08.000
That's Lord for the first plan to hear.

04:08.000 --> 04:15.000
The rest we shall know the joy of thee.

04:16.000 --> 04:41.000
Now that we have found the start of the sermon,

04:41.000 --> 04:45.000
we need to remove all the audio from the start of the recording up to this point.

04:45.000 --> 04:59.000
We can do this with a program menu by going to select, then region, then track start to cursor.

05:00.000 --> 05:11.000
Then, delete the selected audio by selecting edit and then delete.

05:11.000 --> 05:25.000
Verify that the audio starts at the beginning of the sermon by clicking on the skip to start button and then play.

05:25.000 --> 05:46.000
Next, we will use the toolbar button that zooms out to see the entire recording and then search for the end of the sermon.

05:46.000 --> 05:56.000
In the diocese, through Jesus Christ, our Lord, Amen.

06:17.000 --> 06:35.000
To delete the audio from this point to the end of the service, we will go to the menu and choose select and then region and then cursor to track end and then edit and delete.

06:35.000 --> 06:44.000
Then, click the button to zoom out to the entire recording.

06:44.000 --> 06:50.000
One thing we haven't done and that you can do at any time is to save the current state of the recording.

06:50.000 --> 07:04.000
To do this from the menu, select file, then save project and then save project again.

07:04.000 --> 07:08.000
There are a couple more editing steps to take before we are done.

07:08.000 --> 07:14.000
First, we will compress the audio, which will even out some of the louder and softer parts of the recording.

07:14.000 --> 07:21.000
To compress the audio, first make sure either no particular part of the recording is selected or select all of the recording.

07:21.000 --> 07:27.000
Then, from the menu, select affect and then legacy compressor.

07:27.000 --> 07:38.000
Click the presets and settings button, then factory presets and then defaults.

07:38.000 --> 07:51.000
Make sure both boxes are checked at the bottom and click apply.

07:51.000 --> 08:12.000
Then, normalize the audio again using the normalize effect and the same minus 2 dB value as before.

08:12.000 --> 08:19.000
Now we are ready to export the audio to an MP3 file which will then be uploaded to the website.

08:20.000 --> 08:28.000
From the menu, select file, then export audio.

08:28.000 --> 08:38.000
We have been naming the MP3 files with the date of the recording, using a 4-digit year, 2-digit month and 2-digit day to make an 8-digit number.

08:38.000 --> 08:47.000
Make sure the settings match those shown here, MP3 for the format, mono selected for channels.

08:47.000 --> 08:52.000
Bitrate mode set to preset and quality set to standard.

08:52.000 --> 08:56.000
Click the edit metadata button at the lower left.

08:56.000 --> 09:04.000
This allows you to set properties on the MP3 file and we will use the information we copied from the online sermon form.

09:04.000 --> 09:12.000
The artist name and track title are especially useful as the website will automatically read these values when the file is posted to the website.

09:12.000 --> 09:17.000
Fill in the values as shown in this example.

09:42.000 --> 09:54.000
Click OK on the metadata window.

09:54.000 --> 10:03.000
Then, click export.

10:04.000 --> 10:09.000
Now you should have an MP3 file ready to upload to the church website.

10:09.000 --> 10:14.000
You may want to test the MP3 file by opening it in a player program.

10:14.000 --> 10:21.000
In the next video, we will discuss posting the MP3 file to the church website.

